  "a135": {
   "titolo": "Adobe’s AI experiments let you walk around in photos and interview your characters",
   "link": "https://www.theverge.com/tech/1031505/adobe-max-2026-sneaks-experiments-ai",
   "sito": "",
   "data": "2026-11-11T09:00:00-05:00",
   "testo": "Some of the most impressive tech that Adobe showcases at its annual Max conference each year is the experimental features it’s developing, and the latest of those tools are being announced today: they can transform images into explorable 3D scenes, design AI characters by talking to them, and turn photo editing into a copy/paste process. Adobe’s AI experiments let you walk around in photos and interview your characters Here are some of the experimental ‘sneaks’ being showcased at Adobe Max. The first (and weirdest, in my opinion) is Project Screen Test, which is designed to make AI-generated characters easier to develop and use for video content. Instead of just describing how the character should sound and behave in text prompts, the tool lets you talk to the character and direct them in an audition-like environment. You start by describing some base personality traits, such as “anxious, shy, awkward,” and giving the character a voice, either by selecting a preset, describing how it sounds in a prompt, or uploading sample audio for it to reference. You can then expand its persona in further detail by conversing with the character as if you’re talking on FaceTime, outlining its backstory, temperament, goals, flaws, fears, movement quirks and more. Once you’re happy, the tool lets you generate animated screen tests with the character by describing an environment or scenario you want to put them in. This is currently a standalone creative exercise, and it’s not clear how (or if) it could feature in any of Adobe’s existing AI video tools. The results provided a more identifiable and consistent personality compared to many soulless animated AI characters I’ve seen, but that’s the entire reason this exists — AI video generation provides far less creative control compared to…",
   "fonte": "The Verge",
   "tipo": "testata",
   "solo_titolo": false,
   "categoria": "Testate tech",
   "id": "a135",
   "testo_completo": true
  },
  "a68": {
   "titolo": "What a self-hosted dashboard looks like on 31.5 inches of colour e-ink",
   "link": "https://www.reddit.com/r/selfhosted/comments/1xtaf06/what_a_selfhosted_dashboard_looks_like_on_315/",
   "sito": "",
   "data": "2026-11-11T06:18:43+00:00",
   "testo": "I maintain Tesserae, a self-hosted server for e-ink dashboards. You compose a dashboard in the browser, the server renders it headless, and it pushes the frame to your panels. It's AGPL-3.0, runs as a Docker container or Home Assistant add-on, and needs no account. The frame is a Fraimic Large Canvas, a 31.5\" colour e-ink display. Two dashboards are in rotation on it: The project's own numbers fo…",
   "fonte": "Reddit r/selfhosted",
   "tipo": "community",
   "solo_titolo": false,
   "categoria": "Gemme",
   "id": "a68"
  },
  "a163": {
   "titolo": "DeepL wants real-time translation to finally capture sarcasm and mood shifts",
   "link": "https://the-decoder.com/with-its-new-voice-model-deepl-voice-now-translates-intonation-and-speaking-speed-into-other-languages-as-well/",
   "data": "2026-11-11T13:50:30+00:00",
   "testo": "DeepL wants real-time translation to finally capture sarcasm and mood shifts DeepL Voice's real-time translations are about to sound a lot more natural. The company's new Aure model carries over tone, emotion, emphasis, and speaking pace from the original speaker's voice into the target language. Until now, DeepL Voice generated translated speech from a static voice sample captured at the start of a conversation. That meant it couldn't pick up on mood shifts or sarcasm. Aure works differently, continuously analyzing both the translated text and the original audio as the conversation unfolds. During training, DeepL separates voice identity, speech melody, and content so each component can be transferred into the new language independently. Reinforcement learning keeps accents out of the output. Aure runs automatically in DeepL Voice for Online Meetings, In-Person Conversations, and the DeepL API. At launch, the model supports English, German, Spanish, French, Italian, Japanese, Portuguese, and Korean. DeepL first rolled out voice preservation in September. In May, the company cut about 250 jobs to restructure around smaller, AI-powered teams as part of what it called becoming an \"AI-native\" company. DeepL also acquired the team behind audio streaming specialist Mixhalo to bolster its real-time speech translation. Block is chasing a similar goal of capturing meaning beyond words. The company recently unveiled Dessa, a speech model designed to detect emotions and sarcasm directly from audio. AI News Without the Hype – Curated by Humans Subscribe to THE DECODER for ad-free reading, a weekly AI newsletter, our exclusive \"AI Radar\" frontier report six times a year, full archive access, and access to our comment section. Subscribe now",
   "fonte": "The Decoder",
   "tipo": "testata",
   "solo_titolo": false,
   "categoria": "Testate tech",
   "id": "a163",
   "testo_completo": true
  }
 },
